# modules.cpp addressable RGB LEDs

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other specifications. This document specifies the portable
LED interface mm.led, its WS2812B controller, and the mm.mcu pulse facility
beneath it. mm.led, mm.led.ws2812b, the pulse facility with its Pico
implementation, the platform.zero.led provider on the rp2040_zero and
rp2350_zero boards, and the rgb-led-smoke application exist. Light on
hardware remains a hardware-qualification requirement rather than a
completed claim.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the MCU provider model, and
docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy the Pico SDK bridge. docs/modules-lcd.mdy is
the pattern this follows: an interface, a portable controller beneath it, and
a board provider that owns the wiring.

# Separation of responsibilities

Four layers own four different facts:

- mm.mcu owns the pulse transport: one GPIO, bit timing, a reset gap.
- A controller module owns the device's protocol: its timing, its byte order,
  and its frame.
- An LED provider owns the wiring and the length of the chain.
- A board profile states that the provider's LEDs are actually attached.

The Pico SDK adapter knows bit periods and high times, not LEDs. The
controller includes no Pico SDK header. The portable application does not
know which controller or GPIO was selected.

# Portable interface

mm.led::Led is a chain of addressable RGB LEDs; one LED is a chain of one.

Opening fence
struct Color { std::uint8_t red, green, blue; };

class Led {
    unsigned int count() const;
    Status initialize();
    Status write(unsigned int first, std::span<const Color> colors);
    Status refresh();
    Status clear();
    Status sleep();
};
void set_led(Led&);
Led& selected_led();
Closing fence

Every method answers Unsupported by default and count answers zero, so an
executable whose board binds no provider links against the fallback and
learns that at run time. The lifecycle is mm.display's: write changes what
the controller holds, refresh makes it visible. initialize leaves every LED
off; clear turns them off and refreshes; sleep turns them off and returns
the transport. A write or refresh before initialize or after sleep is
NotInitialized, and a run of colours that does not fit the chain is
BadArgument.

Color is red, green, blue in the caller's terms. The order on the wire is the
controller's, and brightness and perceptual correction are the caller's.

# The pulse facility

Opening fence
struct PulseConfiguration {
    unsigned int instance;
    unsigned int gpio;
    std::uint32_t bit_period_ns;
    std::uint32_t zero_high_ns;
    std::uint32_t one_high_ns;
    std::uint32_t reset_ns;
};
Status pulse_configure(const PulseConfiguration&);
Status pulse_write(unsigned int instance, std::span<const std::byte>);
Status pulse_release(unsigned int instance);
Closing fence

Each bit is one period starting high, high for the zero or the one time, low
for the rest; bytes go most significant bit first; after the last bit the
line stays low for at least the reset time. Times must satisfy
0 < zero < one < period. write sends one whole frame and returns once the
reset time has passed. A pad another facility holds is Busy, and a pulse
instance's pad is held as an analog claim is.

The Pico bridge runs each instance, zero and one, on a PIO state machine with
a four-instruction program built at configure:

Opening fence
0: out x, 1     side 0 [t3 - 1]
1: jmp !x 3     side 1 [t1 - 1]
2: jmp 0        side 1 [t2 - 1]
3: nop          side 0 [t2 - 1]
Closing fence

A bit is t3 ticks low, t1 high, then t2 more high for a one or low for a
zero. The bridge chooses the ticks per bit and their split so that both high
times come nearest what was asked within the one-to-sixteen ticks a delay
field holds, and the clock divider that makes those ticks one period. The
WS2812B's 1250, 400, and 800 ns come out exact at 125 and 150 MHz: 25 ticks
of 8, 8, and 9, at a divider of 6.25 or 7.5. The state machine idles stalled
on the out with the line low, takes a byte per FIFO word, and write waits
for the FIFO to empty and then for the last byte and the reset time. Pins
above GP31 are BadArgument, as they are for I2S. Every other platform
answers Unsupported.

# Controller driver

mm.led.ws2812b owns the WS2812B protocol: 1.25 us bits, 0.4 and 0.8 us high
times, 24 bits an LED in green, red, blue order, and a 280 us reset, which
current parts need and the original's 50 us is contained in.

Opening fence
struct Wiring { unsigned int instance; unsigned int gpio; };
enum class Order { Grb, Rgb };
struct Chain { unsigned int count; Order order; std::span<std::byte> frame; };
Closing fence

The frame is provider-owned static storage, three bytes an LED, and must
outlive the Controller; a frame too short for the chain is BadArgument at
initialize. write composes into the frame in wire order and sends nothing;
refresh sends the whole frame. initialize configures the pulse instance and
sends the chain dark, because the LEDs power up showing whatever they took
from the noise on their input. A transport failure during a refresh returns
the controller to Idle, as mm.lcd.st7789 does, because the LEDs' state is
then unknown.

# Provider binding

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.led platform.zero.led
Closing fence

platform.zero.led owns GP16, pulse instance zero, and a one-LED frame, and
registers the controller as mm.led. Existing provider validation,
conditional closure injection, and core platform-edge rules apply unchanged:
an executable that does not reach mm.led carries neither the provider nor the
controller.

# Test strategy

tests/mm/led drives the controller through a recording MCU platform: the
pulse configuration it claims, the dark frame at initialize, GRB and RGB
order, writes at an offset, range and frame-length validation, clear, sleep
and reinitialize, and transport failure. tests/mm/mcu checks that an
unserved platform answers Unsupported for the pulse calls.

scripts/build-rgb-led.sh builds rgb-led-smoke through the real Pico bridge,
checks the pulse adapter symbols are in the image, and checks that
target-smoke-any carries no provider.

# What this does not settle

- Light on hardware is not qualified.
- write blocks with the core feeding the FIFO a byte at a time. A handler
  that holds the core for longer than the FIFO's eight bytes take to send,
  80 us, breaks the frame early; DMA would remove that limit.
- Gamma, brightness, and colour temperature are the caller's.
- Other one-wire LEDs -- SK6812, WS2811, APA106 -- fit the pulse facility
  with their own timing and are not implemented; SPI-clocked LEDs such as the
  APA102 belong on mm.mcu SPI and are not implemented.
